            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Before the new one: say the Latin for sleeplessness, then the Latin for to recline."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: gēns, gentis",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "gēns, gentis — \"a clan, family line; a people\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Haec gēns antīqua et nōbilis est. This clan is old and noble."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Feminine, third declension. A Roman's family name showed his gēns. English gentle and genteel come from it."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "gēns is inherited from a Proto-Indo-European word for \"birth, family\", built on the root of gignō \"to beget\" (EtymDB; Lewis & Short). English gentle and gentile come from it, and English kin and kind are cousins."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "One more word for telling, explaining and describing at B1."
