          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "affīnis, affīne — \"related by marriage; an in-law\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Socer meus affīnis meus est. My father-in-law is my relative by marriage."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Two endings. As a noun, a relative by marriage, as against a cognātus by blood. English affinity."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "affīnis is built on ad- \"at\" and fīnis \"boundary\" (Lewis & Short; EtymDB): first a neighbour, then a relative by marriage. English affinity comes from it."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "notice",
